report='''# Brain6 sleep GWAS power audit (outcome-blinded, current evidence)\n\nAudit date: 2026-09-25. Scope: Brain6 only. Canonical LAVA v3 is read-only and remains `FAILED_QC_NOT_PROMOTED`. A checksum-verified continuous-duration source has now passed Brain6-only normalization QC and been materialized against the frozen 2,495 loci; its trait-only local-h² screen is running. This audit uses the frozen canonical aggregate, its per-trait cause table, and Brain6 GWAS metadata. It does not use or import any frailty/FI sensitivity result.\n\n## Scope correction\n\nThe canonical v3 family has seven inputs, but only two are sleep GWAS: insomnia and long sleep. ADHD, bipolar disorder, MDD, Parkinson disease, and schizophrenia are disorder GWAS. The instruction's reference to “seven sleep phenotypes” is therefore corrected to the two actual Brain6 sleep phenotypes.\n\n## Current-trait technical audit\n\n`current_sleep_trait_audit.tsv` contains source, phenotype, sample, ancestry/build, source variant count, frozen reference-support failures, local-h² distribution, and frozen receipt counts. “TESTED” is processable local-h² output, not significant evidence. The strict frozen per-cell gate is p < 0.05/17,465 = 2.8629e-6; it is shown separately. Median and quartiles describe TESTED loci only. Total variant overlap with the full frozen LD panel is not reported in the Brain6 source/receipt tables; the per-locus min-K failure count is reported instead.\n\nLong sleep is the weakest-powered of the two Brain6 sleep phenotypes by objective local-h² processability: 1,204/2,495 loci tested (48.26%) versus insomnia 1,824/2,495 (73.11%), a 24.85 percentage-point gap. Long sleep has 1,291 NOT_RUN (51.74%), of which 1,271 are low-local-h² and 20 fail shared-reference minimum K; there are no other failures. Insomnia has 671 NOT_RUN (26.89%), of which 651 are low-local-h² and 20 fail shared-reference minimum K. The pattern points primarily to local-h² support, with the same smaller reference-K burden for both traits. Strict univariate p-threshold passes are 1 long-sleep locus and 0 insomnia loci; this stricter count is not the same as processability. Neither result promotes local-rg analysis.\n\n## Candidate search and predeclared decision rule\n\nBefore considering downstream associations, define a materially promising candidate as at least a 10 percentage-point gain in loci passing the frozen strict local-h² p gate (p<2.8629e-6) across the same 2,495 frozen loci (at least 250 additional gate-passing loci). Processability (a finite local-h² result) and strict-gate eligibility are reported separately. This strict-gate improvement only qualifies a candidate for a full sensitivity run; canonical thresholds, the 5% family missingness ceiling, and all downstream gates remain unchanged. A 10-point improvement alone does not pass canonical family QC.\n\nThe continuous Dashti 2019 sleep-duration GWAS is a scientifically related supplement candidate: N=446,118 EUR, GRCh37, same 24-hour self-report study as the canonical >=9-hour tail. The continuous measure and the long-sleep binary tail are different estimands and share UK Biobank participants, so it must not silently replace long sleep or be treated as independent replication. The primary article confirms N=446,118 for continuous duration and 34,184 cases versus 305,742 controls for >=9-hour sleep. Summary statistics are listed through the GWAS Catalog (GCST007561). The source archive (`77ebce3f…d7c8`) and extracted member (`b1115038…fdda0`) were SHA-256 verified; Brain6 normalization retained 6,344,850 unique variants after QC, and reference matching found 6,093,758 (96.04%) across the sealed panel. All 2,495 frozen loci are materialized. The trait-only LAVA screen is running in four disjoint workers under the canonical p threshold and unchanged min-K/local-h² settings. Until it completes, local-h² improvement remains unproven and no full sensitivity run is authorized.\n\nThe Austin-Zimmerman 2023 EUR >=10-hour candidate is a closer binary phenotype but has 15,962 cases and 382,950 controls (effective N≈61,293), about half the canonical long-sleep effective N≈122,985; its stricter threshold further narrows the phenotype. It is not a power improvement. The exact author-listed EUR file's genome build/field semantics and participant overlap also remain unverified. The larger Jansen 1.33M insomnia meta-analysis is not usable as a replacement because the full meta-analysis summary statistics are not publicly available; Brain6 already uses its available UKB-only release.

Watanabe et al. 2022 report a 2,365,010-person European insomnia meta-analysis (593,724 cases; 1,771,286 controls). Its primary data statement makes the full UKB GWAS and only the top 10,000 23andMe SNPs public; full 23andMe data require qualified access, and the full combined meta-analysis is not available. The public UKB component (N=386,988) is essentially the same cohort and sample size as Brain6's current UKB-only insomnia GWAS (N=386,533), so it does not constitute a meaningful power upgrade. It is not used as a replacement.

An Austin-Zimmerman et al. 2025 medRxiv preprint reports a larger continuous-duration analysis across UKB and the Million Veteran Program (646,218 in Results; 648,125 in Abstract/Introduction). The quantitative duration construct is relevant, but the MVP question explicitly includes naps and its response categories differ from UKB. A fresh primary-source check on 2026-09-26 confirms the record remains a preprint: [medRxiv v1 states](https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.05.19.25327902v1) that study-produced data will be available on reasonable request after peer-reviewed publication, and [PubMed indexes it as a preprint](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40475156/). The [current GWAS Catalog sleep-duration search](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/gwas/search?query=sleep+duration) shows the 2023 Austin-Zimmerman paper but no 2025 accession; no independently downloadable 2025 summary-statistics file, build, variant schema, or effect fields could be verified. UKB source overlap with canonical long sleep and the FI GWAS (which uses UKB plus Swedish TwinGene) is known at cohort level; exact participant intersections are unknown. Despite the larger reported N, this candidate fails the current usability and ancestry/reference-compatibility gates. Do not treat it as an available replacement; reconsider only if a checksum-verifiable file is released with documented fields and a defensible ancestry-specific subset.\n\n## Recommendations and runtime summary\n\n- Insomnia: **KEEP** the current UKB-only GWAS; no accessible, technically qualified equivalent with independently verified greater local-h² eligibility is established.\n- Long sleep: **SUPPLEMENT WITH SENSITIVITY** candidate: the source has passed Brain6-only acquisition, harmonization, and reference-coverage checks; proceed to a full sensitivity only if the ongoing trait-only screen improves strict-gate eligible loci by at least 10 percentage points. Keep the canonical >=9-hour result unchanged.\n- Candidates reviewed: 5; potentially higher-N related continuous-duration candidates identified: 2; independently acquired and normalized in Brain6: 1; candidates passing a completed Brain6 local-h² screen: 0 (the continuous-duration screen is running); full bivariate sensitivity families launched/completed: 0; newly eligible downstream loci: 0. No downstream biology was run because canonical v3 failed family QC and no sensitivity candidate passed its technical gate.\n\nThe common-source paper reports continuous duration at N=446,118 and long sleep as >=9 h versus 7–8 h controls. The inaccessible higher-N candidate is described in [Austin-Zimmerman et al. 2025 (preprint)](https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.05.19.25327902v1). The insomnia meta-analysis access limits are described by [Watanabe et al. 2022](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41588-022-01124-w). See also [Dashti et al. 2019](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-019-08917-4), [GWAS Catalog summary-statistics availability](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/gwas/labs/summary-statistics), [Jansen et al. 2019](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41588-018-0333-3), and [Austin-Zimmerman et al. 2023](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-023-41249-y).\n'''
(OUT/'README.md').write_text(report)
